 "findings": [
  "HTTP_PROXY, HTTPS_PROXY and the lower-case http_proxy are honoured by the hook client: with one of them set and no matching NO_PROXY entry, every hook request goes through the proxy (both versions).",
  "ALL_PROXY and all_proxy are NOT honoured by the hook client: the hook request goes direct although the model requests of the same CLI go through the proxy (both versions). So ALL_PROXY alone does not put hooks behind a proxy, though the C-2 channel rule may still treat it as a proxy to stay safe.",
  "NO_PROXY is honoured for an exact host (127.0.0.1, localhost), for * and for a .suffix (.localhost matched the host localhost); the lower-case no_proxy is honoured too. A name does not match an address: NO_PROXY=localhost does not exempt a hook URL on 127.0.0.1.",
  "An IPv6 loopback entry in NO_PROXY is NOT honoured: with NO_PROXY set to ::1 or [::1] (upper or lower case) the hook request to http://[::1]:port still goes through the proxy (both versions). The hook URL must therefore never use [::1].",
  "A host:port entry that does not carry the real port does not match (case p19, a deliberately wrong port; no claim is made about matching a correct port)."
 ],
 "notes": [
  "Each case is one plain turn with hooks for UserPromptSubmit and Stop on an http receiver on 127.0.0.1 and ::1. A forwarding recording proxy on 127.0.0.1 passes everything on, so the CLI works in every case, and logs the target of every request.",
  "The model API stub is on 127.0.0.1 too, so cases without a NO_PROXY entry also send model requests through the proxy; that is why those columns differ."
 ]
}
